---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Graphic from '../components/Graphic.astro';
import Text from '../components/Text.astro';

// Real copy, pulled from the original course's Introduction page in full
// (course/en/components.json, articles under page id
// 6514457691935905f0aca85f) - previously only "Going beyond the law" was
// ported; the demo Accordion/Mcq borrowed from Data Collection have been
// removed (they were only ever there to prove the components worked
// before Data Collection had its own real page).
//
// The ODI definition and the "three areas of focus" it cites (data /
// algorithms / practices) used to sit together in one paragraph, which read
// as though the course itself was structured around those three areas -
// it isn't; every page from here on is organised around the data lifecycle
// instead. That three-areas framing is still genuinely useful (it's how
// the wider field of data ethics is often described), so it's kept, but
// pulled into its own "wider view" aside rather than the main narrative,
// so it reads as context rather than a structural promise the course
// doesn't keep.
const introBody = `<p>The <a class="customlink" href="https://theodi.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><u>Open Data Institute</u></a> defines <strong>data ethics</strong> as 'a branch of ethics that evaluates data practices with the potential to adversely impact on people and society – in data collection, sharing, and use.' It argues that data ethics is not only relevant to how organisations use, process, or analyse data, but the impact of data activities at every stage of the <strong>data lifecycle</strong> – which is exactly what this course walks through, stage by stage.</p><p>It's not just <strong>right </strong>to handle data responsibly – the UK GDPR and the <a class="customlink" href="https://www.gov.uk/data-protection" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><u>Data Protection Act 2018</u></a> make it the <strong>law</strong>. Together, these set out strict rules – called data protection principles – that everyone who uses personal data must follow. But meeting a legal requirement is just one part of data ethics. It doesn't always follow that an organisation is behaving ethically just because it's compliant with the law.</p>`;

const wideningBody = `<p>Data ethics as a field covers more ground than the lifecycle stages this course walks through. Its <a class="customlink" href="https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rsta.2016.0360" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><u>main areas of focus</u></a> are often described as:</p><ul><li>The ethics of data (e.g., collection, processing and use)</li><li>The ethics of algorithms (e.g., use of artificial intelligence)</li><li>The ethics of practices (e.g., responsible innovation, user privacy)</li></ul><p>All three surface at different points across the stages ahead – worth keeping in mind as a wider lens on top of them, not a checklist this course works through separately.</p>`;

const comingUpBody = `<p>The first stage of the data lifecycle we'll cover is <strong>data collection</strong>. The law states that organisations must act transparently, with consent, collecting data only for the explicit purpose it's needed. But above and beyond legal requirements, there's still more you can do to serve customers ethically and responsibly during data collection.</p><p><br><strong>Select Next to continue to the next section.</strong></p>`;
---

<Layout title="Introduction" duration="2 minutes.">
  <Graphic src="/assets/introduction-banner.png" alt="Introduction" />

  <Text title="Going beyond the law" body={introBody} />

  <div class="a8-aside">
    <p class="a8-aside__eyebrow">Zooming out</p>
    <Text body={wideningBody} />
  </div>

  <Graphic
    src="/assets/introduction-quote.png"
    alt="Think of it as the difference between what you could do and what you should do."
  />

  <Text title="Coming up..." body={comingUpBody} />
</Layout>

<style>
  /* Sets the "three areas of focus" apart from the main narrative - same
     left-accent-border convention already used for supplementary panels
     elsewhere (e.g. Hotgraphic's detail panel), so it reads as a wider-
     context aside rather than another equally-weighted section. */
  .a8-aside {
    background: var(--a8-surface);
    border: 1px solid #ddd;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--a8-accent);
    border-radius: var(--a8-radius);
    padding: var(--a8-space-md) var(--a8-space-md) 0;
    margin-bottom: var(--a8-space-lg);
  }
  .a8-aside__eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 var(--a8-space-sm);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--a8-text-muted);
  }
  .a8-aside :global(.a8-text) {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    padding-bottom: var(--a8-space-md);
  }
</style>
